From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valentin rameau <valentin.rameau@outlook.fr>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 06:43:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 28/33] PPU: per-column Mode 7 margin tiles, and a switch to
 skip the centre

Two hooks a widescreen host needs, both drawing-only and both bound for the
duration of one ppu_runLine, exactly like renderVram.

m7MarginTiles is a borrowed uint8_t per widescreen margin COLUMN. A host that
widens a Mode 7 view samples course positions the game never uploaded -- the
128x128 tilemap wraps, so those cells hold tiles from elsewhere -- and until
now the only channel back into the rasteriser was the tilemap itself. That
channel is lossy: 16,384 cells are time-shared between the margin columns, so
two columns on one line can need different tiles from one cell, and a host
using it has to detect the collision and render the line twice. One slot per
column cannot collide. It is a per-SPAN choice, not a per-pixel branch: the
span list is split at x=0 and x=256 and the margin spans get a second copy of
the inner loop differing only in where the tile number comes from, because
this is the largest per-pixel loop here and it is already short of registers.
The out-of-field bypass is kept verbatim -- where (xpos|ypos) > outside_value
the hardware reads no tilemap cell, so neither tile source may be consulted.

wsCentreSkip marks the authentic 256-wide centre as already drawn, for a host
that renders the frame twice (once at 256 for the authentic output, once wider
for the margins) and discards the wider render's centre. Every drawer already
opens with a test on the span bits, so they convert for free. Two sites do not:
ClearBackdrop, which becomes two ranges -- both bounds are multiples of 4, so
they land on the same uint64 stores the single range did -- and the compose
loop, which cannot take it from the bits because those are the COLOUR window's
and the margins still read them for clip and colour math. Layer 5 is therefore
split but never marked, and the compose loop drops the span on its bounds,
advancing dst past what it did not write.

Splitting a span is exact because every drawer re-seeds its source coordinates
from win.edges[windex] at the top of each span -- the Mode 7 affine chain
included -- rather than carrying them across the boundary.

PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe decides per scanline whether the skip leaves the
margins unchanged. It refuses on horizontal mosaic, which averages across x and
is the only coupling inside the drawers, and on every per-line widescreen
policy that rewrites the span list. All of those are inert unless a host asked
for them, so the failure mode when one is not is a slower line, not a wrong one.

Proven byte-identical on the F-Zero host over 2,999 frames spanning 1,707
distinct wide images and 1,706 distinct native framebuffers, with each hook
exercised alone and both together.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 runner/src/snes/ppu.c | 132 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 runner/src/snes/ppu.h |  43 ++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 175 insertions(+)

diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
index 83f9b0e..ad51cba 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
@@ -331,6 +331,48 @@ void PpuSetExtraSpace(Ppu *ppu, uint16_t extra) {
   PpuResetLayerPolicies(ppu);
 }
 
+void PpuSetMode7MarginTiles(Ppu *ppu, const uint8_t *tiles) {
+  ppu->m7MarginTiles = tiles;
+}
+
+void PpuSetWidescreenCentreSkip(Ppu *ppu, bool skip) {
+  ppu->wsCentreSkip = skip ? 1 : 0;
+}
+
+bool PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe(const Ppu *ppu, int y) {
+  // There has to BE a margin, or "skip the centre" means "draw nothing".
+  if (!(ppu->extraLeftCur | ppu->extraRightCur))
+    return false;
+  // Horizontal mosaic averages across x, so a margin pixel can be decided by
+  // a centre pixel. This is the only coupling in the drawers themselves.
+  if ((ppu->mosaic & 0x0f) && PPU_mosaicSize(ppu) > 1)
+    return false;
+  // Everything below rewrites the span list per line. None of them is wrong
+  // in principle, but each would have to be reasoned about against the split,
+  // and all are inert unless a host asked for them.
+  if (ppu->widescreenLineEnhancer || ppu->wsMode2CaptureLayer ||
+      ppu->wsHudSplitHeight || ppu->wsHudOamHeight ||
+      ppu->wsWindowExpandLayers)
+    return false;
+  if (PpuWidescreenLineRepeatBandActive(ppu, y))
+    return false;
+  for (unsigned int layer = 0; layer < 4; layer++) {
+    if (ppu->wsMarginGapL[layer] | ppu->wsMarginGapR[layer] |
+        ppu->wsViewportInsetL[layer] | ppu->wsViewportInsetR[layer])
+      return false;
+    if (PpuWidescreenLayerWorldBandAt(ppu, layer, y) >= 0 ||
+        PpuWidescreenLayerElasticBandAt(ppu, layer, y) >= 0)
+      return false;
+    for (unsigned int slot = 0; slot < kPpuWsAnchorBands; slot++) {
+      if (y >= ppu->wsAnchorY0[slot][layer] &&
+          y < ppu->wsAnchorY1[slot][layer] &&
+          ppu->wsAnchorLeftEnd[slot][layer])
+        return false;
+    }
+  }
+  return true;
+}
+
 void PpuSetExtraSpaceCentered(Ppu *ppu, uint16_t budget) {
   if (budget > kPpuExtraLeftRight)
     budget = kPpuExtraLeftRight;
@@ -693,6 +735,19 @@ static inline void PpuActiveSpan(const Ppu *ppu, size_t *begin,
 static inline void ClearBackdrop(const Ppu *ppu, PpuPixelPrioBufs *buf) {
   size_t begin, end;
   PpuActiveSpan(ppu, &begin, &end);
+  // With the centre skipped nothing writes it and nothing reads it, so
+  // clearing it is 64% of this memset spent on pixels that are discarded.
+  // Both bounds are multiples of 4 (kPpuExtraLeftRight is 272 and kPpuXPixels
+  // 256), so the two ranges land on the same uint64 stores as the whole one.
+  if (ppu->wsCentreSkip) {
+    size_t centre_begin = (size_t)kPpuExtraLeftRight;
+    size_t centre_end = (size_t)kPpuExtraLeftRight + kPpuXPixels;
+    for (size_t i = begin; i < centre_begin; i += 4)
+      *(uint64*)&buf->data[i] = 0x0500050005000500;
+    for (size_t i = centre_end; i < end; i += 4)
+      *(uint64*)&buf->data[i] = 0x0500050005000500;
+    return;
+  }
   for (size_t i = begin; i < end; i += 4)
     *(uint64*)&buf->data[i] = 0x0500050005000500;
 }
@@ -805,12 +860,42 @@ typedef struct PpuWindows {
 // clamped to the authentic 256-wide region so a BG3 status bar never tiles into
 // the margins -- EXCEPT on scanlines >= wsBg3WidenY, where the game renders
 // level content on BG3 (e.g. SMW water) that should fill 16:9 like BG1/BG2.
+static void PpuWindowsSplit(PpuWindows *win, int16 *bias, int xpos);
+
+// Split the span list at the authentic screen edges and mark everything
+// between them as already drawn. Splitting is exact: every drawer re-seeds its
+// source coordinates from win.edges[windex] at the top of each span -- the
+// Mode 7 affine chain included -- so two spans render the same pixels one
+// span would. Only the bits change what is drawn.
+//
+// LAYER 5 IS THE COLOUR WINDOW and is deliberately left unmarked: the compose
+// loop reads cwin.bits to decide clip and colour-math per span, so setting a
+// bit there would change the MARGINS. The compose loop does its own skip on
+// the span bounds instead, which is why the split still happens for layer 5.
+//
+// The bias array only matters for the per-line policies that use it, and
+// PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe refuses the whole scheme when any of those is
+// live -- so the caller's bias is all zeroes and a scratch one is equivalent.
+static void PpuApplyCentreSkip(PpuWindows *win, uint layer) {
+  int16 bias[8] = { 0 };
+  PpuWindowsSplit(win, bias, 0);
+  PpuWindowsSplit(win, bias, kPpuXPixels);
+  if (layer == 5)
+    return;
+  for (uint i = 0; i < win->nr; i++) {
+    if (win->edges[i] >= 0 && win->edges[i + 1] <= kPpuXPixels)
+      win->bits |= (uint8)(1u << i);
+  }
+}
+
 static void PpuWindows_Clear(PpuWindows *win, Ppu *ppu, uint layer, int y) {
   win->edges[0] = -PpuWidescreenLayerExtra(ppu, layer, y, ppu->extraLeftCur);
   win->edges[1] = 256 + PpuWidescreenLayerExtra(ppu, layer, y,
                                                 ppu->extraRightCur);
   win->nr = 1;
   win->bits = 0;
+  if (ppu->wsCentreSkip)
+    PpuApplyCentreSkip(win, layer);
 }
 
 static void PpuWindows_CalcWithExtra(PpuWindows *win, Ppu *ppu, uint layer,
@@ -903,6 +988,8 @@ static void PpuWindows_CalcWithExtra(PpuWindows *win, Ppu *ppu, uint layer,
   } else {
     win->bits = w1_bits | w2_bits;
   }
+  if (ppu->wsCentreSkip)
+    PpuApplyCentreSkip(win, layer);
   debug_server_on_ppu_window(y, (int)layer, win->edges, win->nr, win->bits);
 }
 
@@ -2011,6 +2098,19 @@ static void PpuDrawBackground_mode7(Ppu *ppu, PpuPixelPrioBufs *dstbuf, uint y,
    * measure nothing, so its worth is a bg+mode7 delta across builds. */
   const uint16_t *const m7vram = PpuRenderVram(ppu);
 #define M7VRAM m7vram
+  /* Host-supplied margin tiles (see Ppu::m7MarginTiles). Splitting the span
+   * list at the authentic screen edges turns "which tile source" from a test
+   * on every pixel into one test per span -- which matters here, because this
+   * is the largest per-pixel loop in the renderer and it is already short of
+   * registers. The split is exact for the same reason the centre skip is: the
+   * affine chain below re-seeds from win.edges[windex] rather than carrying
+   * xpos/ypos across the boundary. */
+  const uint8_t *const m7margin = ppu->m7MarginTiles;
+  if (m7margin && !mosaic_enabled && !ppu->wsCentreSkip) {
+    int16 split_bias[8] = { 0 };
+    PpuWindowsSplit(&win, split_bias, 0);
+    PpuWindowsSplit(&win, split_bias, kPpuXPixels);
+  }
   for (size_t windex = 0; windex < win.nr; windex++) {
     if (win.bits & (1 << windex))
       continue;  // layer is disabled for this window part
@@ -2041,6 +2141,28 @@ static void PpuDrawBackground_mode7(Ppu *ppu, PpuPixelPrioBufs *dstbuf, uint y,
           do dstz[i] = pixel + z; while (++i != w);
         }
       } while (xpos += dx * w, ypos += dy * w, dstz += w, w = PPU_mosaicSize(ppu), dstz_end - dstz != 0);
+    } else if (m7margin && (x2 <= 0 || x >= kPpuXPixels)) {
+      /* A margin span. The tile number comes from the host's per-column array
+       * instead of the tilemap word; everything else -- the outside-field
+       * test, char fill, the character fetch and the priority store -- is the
+       * loop above, unchanged. The out-of-field branch must still bypass it,
+       * because that is exactly where the hardware does not read a tilemap
+       * cell at all. */
+      const uint8_t *mt = m7margin +
+          (x2 <= 0 ? x + ppu->extraLeftRight
+                   : x - kPpuXPixels + ppu->extraLeftRight);
+      do {
+        if ((uint32)(xpos | ypos) > outside_value) {
+          if (!char_fill)
+            continue;
+          tile = 0;
+        } else {
+          tile = *mt;
+        }
+        uint8 pixel = M7VRAM[tile * 64 + (ypos >> 8 & 7) * 8 + (xpos >> 8 & 7)] >> 8;
+        if (pixel)
+          dstz[0] = pixel + z;
+      } while (xpos += dx, ypos += dy, ++mt, ++dstz != dstz_end);
     } else {
       do {
         if ((uint32)(xpos | ypos) > outside_value) {
@@ -2399,6 +2521,16 @@ static NOINLINE void PpuDrawWholeLine(Ppu *ppu, uint y) {
   uint32 windex = 0;
   do {
     uint32 left = cwin.edges[windex] + kPpuExtraLeftRight, right = cwin.edges[windex + 1] + kPpuExtraLeftRight;
+    /* The centre skip's second hand-done site (the first is ClearBackdrop).
+     * The drawers take it from the span BITS, but cwin's bits carry clip and
+     * colour-math state that the margins still need, so this span is dropped
+     * on its bounds instead. dst walks with the pixel loop rather than being
+     * indexed, so it has to be advanced past the span that was not written. */
+    if (ppu->wsCentreSkip && left >= (uint32)kPpuExtraLeftRight &&
+        right <= (uint32)(kPpuExtraLeftRight + kPpuXPixels)) {
+      dst += right - left;
+      continue;
+    }
     /* The per-pixel widescreen-HUD blanking test depends only on i and on
      * booleans already fixed for this scanline. If no full-width HUD is
      * active AND this span lies entirely inside the world, it can never
diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
index 9946673..459b880 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
@@ -329,6 +329,29 @@ struct Ppu {
   /* Optional host picture memory. Drawing only: CPU ports and save states
    * always use vram. The caller owns 32768 words until unbound. */
   const uint16_t *renderVram;
+  /* Optional host-supplied Mode 7 tilemap IDs for the widescreen margins.
+   * When bound, a Mode 7 pixel whose screen x falls outside [0,256) takes its
+   * tile number from here -- index x+extraLeftRight on the left, and
+   * x-256+extraLeftRight on the right -- instead of from the tilemap word in
+   * picture memory. It exists because a host that widens a Mode 7 view samples
+   * course positions the game never uploaded: the 128x128 tilemap wraps, so
+   * those cells hold tiles from elsewhere. Supplying the correct tile per
+   * MARGIN COLUMN is exact where patching the tilemap is not, because 16,384
+   * cells are time-shared between the margin columns and two columns on one
+   * line can need different tiles from one cell.
+   *
+   * Drawing only, like renderVram: nothing else reads it, and emulated state
+   * is never touched. The caller owns 2*extraLeftRight bytes until unbound. */
+  const uint8_t *m7MarginTiles;
+  /* Skip the authentic 256-wide centre. A host that renders a widened frame
+   * twice -- once at 256 for the authentic output, once wider for the margins
+   * -- discards the wider render's centre. With this set every window span
+   * list is split at x=0 and x=256 and the spans inside that range are marked
+   * as already drawn, so the drawers and the compose loop skip them. Only ever
+   * valid when the caller genuinely discards those pixels; see
+   * PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe for the per-line conditions that make the
+   * remaining margins byte-identical. */
+  uint8_t wsCentreSkip;
   uint32_t overlayRenderPitch[kPpuOverlaySource_Count];
   uint8_t *overlayRenderBuffer[kPpuOverlaySource_Count];
   uint8_t brightnessMult[32 + 31];
@@ -649,6 +672,26 @@ bool PpuSetOverlayOamRange(Ppu *ppu, uint8_t first, uint8_t count);
 // extraRightCur/extraLeftRight machinery used by the line renderer.
 void PpuSetExtraSpace(Ppu *ppu, uint16_t extra);
 
+// Bind (or unbind, with NULL) the per-margin-column Mode 7 tile IDs described
+// at Ppu::m7MarginTiles. The array holds 2*extraLeftRight bytes: the left
+// margin's columns in screen order first, then the right margin's.
+void PpuSetMode7MarginTiles(Ppu *ppu, const uint8_t *tiles);
+
+
+// Ask for the authentic centre to be skipped on the lines where it is safe.
+// See Ppu::wsCentreSkip. Set it per scanline, after PpuSetExtraSpace* (which
+// does not touch it), and only when PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe agrees.
+void PpuSetWidescreenCentreSkip(Ppu *ppu, bool skip);
+
+// Whether skipping the centre on scanline `y` leaves the MARGINS unchanged.
+// False whenever something couples a centre pixel to a margin pixel, or
+// whenever a per-line widescreen policy rewrites the span list in a way the
+// split would fight with: horizontal mosaic (it averages across x), the Mode 2
+// offset-per-tile capture, the line enhancer, the split HUD, margin gaps,
+// anchor/elastic/world/repeat/stretch bands and viewport insets. Every one of
+// those is inert in the ordinary case, so this is a handful of scalar loads.
+bool PpuWidescreenCentreSkipSafe(const Ppu *ppu, int y);
+
 // Render authentic 256-wide content centered within a `budget`-per-side wider
 // framebuffer (no border columns drawn). For bounded screens; caller blacks
 // out the side margins to pillarbox.
